\fs20\b ILLUSTRATIVE EXAMPLE \'96 NOT LEGAL ADVICE.\b0\par
A working checklist, not a court form. Route through your legal services and the magistrates' court. Source: Thinking About Policing.\par
\par
\fs28\b CLOSURE NOTICE & ORDER \'96 CHECKLIST\b0\fs20\par
\i Anti-social Behaviour, Crime and Policing Act 2014, Part 4 Chapter 3 (sections 76\'9693)\i0\par
\par
\b Grounds for a closure notice (s.76)\b0\par
\tab [ ] Reasonable belief that the use of the premises has resulted, or (if the notice is not issued) is likely to result, in nuisance to the public; or\par
\tab [ ] That there has been, or is likely to be, disorder near those premises associated with their use.\par
\par
\b Authorisation\b0\par
\tab [ ] Police officer of at least inspector rank, or the local authority.\par
\tab [ ] Reasonable steps taken to consult and to inform those who appear to live on, own or have control of the premises.\par
\par
\b The notice\b0\par
\tab [ ] Duration up to 48 hours (extendable in limited circumstances).\par
\tab [ ] State clearly who may still enter (e.g. a person who habitually lives there, the owner).\par
\par
\b The order (apply to the magistrates' court)\b0\par
\tab [ ] Application made within 48 hours of the notice being served.\par
\tab [ ] Order may close the premises for up to 3 months, extendable to a maximum of 6 months.\par
\par
\b Safeguarding (do not skip this)\b0\par
\tab [ ] Is the occupant being exploited (cuckooing / county lines)? They are a victim \'96 plan housing, social care and support referrals before and after closure.\par
\par
\b Breach and evidence\b0\par
\tab [ ] Remaining on, or entering, closed premises without reasonable excuse is a criminal offence.\par
\tab [ ] Evidence pack: logs, intelligence, statements, community impact, photographs.\par
